### thermal evidence, second pass, zl1, 2026-09-22
### Context: doc 72 said "~3.0 of 4 cores busy, 2.4 of them in the kernel, no conclusion".
### This pass measures the same thing again, two changes later, and resolves it.
### All numbers are verbatim from /proc/stat and /proc/<pid>; nothing here is inferred
### except where a line says "inference".

===== 1. what changed between the two passes =====
  a) the v63 debug keeper (/usr/local/sbin/zl1-debug-net.sh, pid 817) was SIGSTOPped after doc 72
     (its unit thinks it exited, so systemctl cannot manage it; SIGSTOP is the only handle)
  b) scaling_governor went performance -> interactive on all four cores
     (install-cpufreq-governor.sh, installed as a boot unit)
  c) the container's sensors HAL was restarted once during this pass (ctl.stop / ctl.start,
     A/B below) and is running again

===== 2. A/B of the keeper, 15 s windows, same everything else =====
method: /proc/stat delta; busy = (total - idle)/total * 4; reloads counted from the journal

K0  keeper STOPPED (kill -STOP 817)
    user=5.6%  sys=15.0%  idle=78.3%   -> busy = 0.87 cores    reloads in window: 0

K1  keeper RUNNING (kill -CONT 817, then measured)
    user=11.6%  sys=32.7%  idle=54.0%  -> busy = 1.84 cores    reloads in window: 1

    => the keeper costs **0.97 cores**, i.e. ~24% of this 4-core SoC, on top of everything else.
       (The reload count is the weaker number: `journalctl -n 3000` truncates, and the storm's
        measured cadence from doc 72 is `Reloading finished in 2044 ms` every ~6 s, i.e. ~2 per
        15 s. The CPU number is the robust one.)
    => back to STOPPED immediately after: pid 817 state=T.

===== 3. the load average is not CPU: it is kernel threads parked in driver sleeps =====
load average: 5.02 5.35 5.54        while only 0.87 cores were busy and iowait was 0%

process states on the host: {S: 586, R: 3, D: 5, T: 1, Z: 3}
the 5 D-state tasks, with wchan and stack:
  pid 45       (workqueue worker)  process_one_work -> worker_thread -> kthread
  pid 154      mdss_dsi_event      wchan dsi_event_thread
  pid 415      msm-core:sampling   wchan do_sampling
  pid 932451   mdss_fb0            wchan __mdss_fb_display_thread
  pid 3509113  kworker/3:0         wchan usleep_range, stack tsens_poll -> process_one_work

  read: all five are normal periodic kernel work (display event/frame threads, CPU+thermal
  sampling, thermal sensor polling). They sleep inside driver calls, which this kernel accounts
  as D, so they inflate the load average while consuming ~0% CPU.
  => "load 5-6" on this device means "a handful of driver threads are sleeping", NOT "5 cores
     of work". Do not read it as heat.
  => and the container is not the source either: container states {D: 0, R: 0, S: 118}.

===== 4. the sensors HAL A/B was inconclusive at this granularity =====
  A (HAL running, before):   user=9.2%  sys=21.3%  idle=68.4%
  B (ctl.stop vendor.sensors-hal-1-0, 6 s later): user=4.3%  sys=12.6%  idle=82.0%  -> busy 0.72
  C (ctl.start, HAL back):   user=5.5%  sys=13.9%  idle=79.4%  -> busy 0.82
  read: A is not comparable to B/C -- A was taken immediately after several journalctl greps on
  this device, which are themselves a CPU spike. B vs C differ by 0.1 cores, i.e. within the
  noise of a 15 s window. **No conclusion**: the HAL costs something, but this method cannot say
  how much. (Killing it is not a fix anyway -- the accelerometer/magnetometer/gyroscope stream
  through it, doc 71.)

